Make the pipe backend emit little-endian S16.

shairport-sync's pipe backend writes the decoded audio buffer verbatim, in the
host's native byte order. On a big-endian target (ath79 / mips_24kc BE) that is
big-endian S16, but Snapcast's pipe source — and virtually every other pipe
consumer — expects little-endian S16LE. The mismatch turns music into full-scale
white-noise static (every sample's two bytes swapped).

Byte-swap each 16-bit sample before the write. The block compiles out on
little-endian hosts via __BYTE_ORDER__, so it is a no-op everywhere except the
big-endian build and is safe to carry as a downstream patch.

--- a/audio_pipe.c
+++ b/audio_pipe.c
@@ -76,6 +76,17 @@
   }
   // if it's got a reader, write to it.
   if (fd > 0) {
+    // The buffer is native-endian S16 stereo. Snapcast (and most pipe consumers)
+    // expect little-endian S16LE; on a big-endian host that mismatch renders as
+    // white-noise static. Byte-swap each 16-bit sample. Compiles out on LE hosts.
+#if defined(__BYTE_ORDER__) && (__BYTE_ORDER__ == __ORDER_BIG_ENDIAN__)
+    {
+      uint16_t *sp = (uint16_t *)buf;
+      int i, n = samples * 2; /* two int16 samples per stereo frame */
+      for (i = 0; i < n; i++)
+        sp[i] = (uint16_t)((sp[i] << 8) | (sp[i] >> 8));
+    }
+#endif
     // int rc = non_blocking_write(fd, buf, samples * 4);
     int rc = write(fd, buf, samples * 4);
     if ((rc < 0) && (errno != EPIPE)) {
